From d01a026f73060db90afc9af2ea986549da41a715 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yury Klushin <digger@live.ru>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 16:48:12 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] rt11: translate '\r' to '\n' in _read() so fgets() sees line
 endings

RT-11's own console driver terminates an input line with '\r', never
'\n'. newlib's fgets() (refill.c) looks for '\n' specifically via
memchr() to decide a line is complete; the previous code stored '\r'
as-is and only used it (ORed with '\n') as this loop's own terminator,
so fgets() never actually saw a '\n' in the buffer and looped around
for another refill() -- silently stalling past the Enter that had
already terminated the line, then swallowing whatever key came next
into the same buffer.

Translate '\r' to '\n' as each character comes in, mirroring the
LF->CRLF translation write.c already does symmetrically on output.
Safe for any caller that still checks for '\r' too (e.g.
strcspn(buf, "\r\n")) -- '\n' alone already satisfies that check.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 newlib/libc/sys/rt11/read.c | 20 +++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 19 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/newlib/libc/sys/rt11/read.c b/newlib/libc/sys/rt11/read.c
index a200b4711..0cdee7695 100644
--- a/newlib/libc/sys/rt11/read.c
+++ b/newlib/libc/sys/rt11/read.c
@@ -29,8 +29,26 @@ _read (int file, void *ptr, size_t len)
 	{
 	  char c = rt11_ttyin ();
 
+	  /* RT-11's own console driver terminates a line with '\r', never
+	     '\n' -- but newlib's fgets() (refill.c/fgets.c) looks for '\n'
+	     specifically via memchr() to decide a line is complete.  Left
+	     untranslated, a line read back as "TEXT\r" (no '\n' anywhere)
+	     doesn't satisfy that search, so fgets() loops around for
+	     *another* refill(), blocking on one more real keystroke it
+	     will never get from this line -- confirmed directly: it stalls
+	     waiting past the Enter that already terminated the line,
+	     silently swallowing whatever key comes next into the same
+	     buffer.  Translating '\r' to '\n' here mirrors the LF->CRLF
+	     translation write.c already does symmetrically on output (the
+	     canonical terminal-driver "icrnl" behavior RT-11 itself
+	     doesn't provide) and fixes fgets() outright.  Safe for any
+	     caller that still checks for '\r' too (e.g. strcspn(buf,
+	     "\r\n")) -- '\n' alone already satisfies that check.  */
+	  if (c == '\r')
+	    c = '\n';
+
 	  p[i] = c;
-	  if (c == '\r' || c == '\n')
+	  if (c == '\n')
 	    {
 	      i++;
 	      break;
